1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,450
Yaacob Mohamed: The Forgotten Pioneer

2
00:00:03,450 --> 00:00:08,188
When Yaacob Mohamed stepped down
from Parliament in December 1980,

3
00:00:08,268 --> 00:00:13,371
Prime Minister Lee Kuan Yew paid
tribute to him for his part in winning

4
00:00:13,451 --> 00:00:18,050
Malay support for the People's
Action Party in the early 1960s.

5
00:00:18,050 --> 00:00:22,457
Yaacob had left UMNO for the
PAP in 1958, taking 30 of its

6
00:00:22,537 --> 00:00:27,176
members with him, and went on
to serve as a minister of state

7
00:00:27,256 --> 00:00:31,975
and as Singapore's envoy to the
Philippines, India and Egypt.

8
00:00:31,975 --> 00:00:35,938
He also represented the Southern
Islands, whose villages and

9
00:00:36,018 --> 00:00:40,049
schools have since disappeared,
and today he is far less well

10
00:00:40,129 --> 00:00:44,375
known than many of the first-generation
leaders he worked with.

11
00:00:44,375 --> 00:00:48,432
Yaacob bin Mohamed was
born on the 25th of January

12
00:00:48,512 --> 00:00:51,246
1925 at Sungai Pisang in Kelantan,

13
00:00:51,326 --> 00:00:56,125
and his family moved to Johor
when he was still an infant.

14
00:00:56,125 --> 00:01:00,625
His mother died when he was eight
and his father when he was twelve.

15
00:01:00,625 --> 00:01:03,846
Several teachers helped
him to stay in school,

16
00:01:03,926 --> 00:01:06,725
and one of them eventually adopted him.

17
00:01:06,725 --> 00:01:09,475
He often came top of his class.

18
00:01:09,475 --> 00:01:12,656
He wanted to be a
teacher, and he came first

19
00:01:12,736 --> 00:01:15,775
in the examination for teachers in Johor.

20
00:01:15,775 --> 00:01:20,080
His application to the Malay
teachers' college at Tanjong Malim,

21
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:25,025
in Perak, was still turned down,
because he had not been born in Johor.

22
00:01:25,025 --> 00:01:29,249
Deeply disappointed, he moved to
Singapore, where he lived with

23
00:01:29,329 --> 00:01:33,975
relatives and studied at the Madrasah
Khairiyah, a religious school.

24
00:01:33,975 --> 00:01:38,450
The rejection added to his
unhappiness with British rule.

25
00:01:38,450 --> 00:01:42,758
In Johor he joined the left-wing
Malay Nationalist Party

26
00:01:42,838 --> 00:01:47,225
and its youth wing, Angkatan
Pemuda Insaf, known as API.

27
00:01:47,225 --> 00:01:51,462
When the British banned these
groups in the late 1940s,

28
00:01:51,542 --> 00:01:57,114
he returned to Singapore, joined the
United Malays National Organisation

29
00:01:57,194 --> 00:02:01,825
(UMNO) in 1949 and became head
of its Bukit Panjang branch.

30
00:02:01,825 --> 00:02:04,516
The PAP was founded in 1954,

31
00:02:04,596 --> 00:02:08,950
and Yaacob was invited
to join it that year.

32
00:02:08,950 --> 00:02:10,625
He declined.

33
00:02:10,625 --> 00:02:13,926
At the time he believed
that UMNO's talks with the

34
00:02:14,006 --> 00:02:17,509
British over Malaya's
independence would also lead to

35
00:02:17,589 --> 00:02:21,375
independence for Singapore,
through a merger of the two.

36
00:02:21,375 --> 00:02:24,617
He changed his mind
when UMNO indicated that

37
00:02:24,697 --> 00:02:29,000
independence for Singapore
was not one of its priorities.

38
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,450
In 1958 he joined the PAP,

39
00:02:31,530 --> 00:02:35,325
and 30 other UMNO members followed him.

40
00:02:35,325 --> 00:02:41,981
The Founders' Memorial describes 31
Singapore UMNO leaders switching overnight,

41
00:02:42,061 --> 00:02:44,875
and dates the move to early 1959.

42
00:02:44,875 --> 00:02:50,075
UMNO condemned the defectors as
traitors to the Malay community.

43
00:02:50,075 --> 00:02:53,410
Yaacob, a devout Muslim
and a forceful speaker,

44
00:02:53,490 --> 00:02:57,842
quickly won the trust of the
PAP's leaders, and within months

45
00:02:57,922 --> 00:03:01,700
he was chairman of the
party's Bukit Panjang branch.

46
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:05,452
In the general election
of May 1959 he stood

47
00:03:05,532 --> 00:03:09,625
for Bukit Timah, a mainly
Chinese constituency.

48
00:03:09,625 --> 00:03:13,485
He won 6,174 votes, or 61.1 per cent,

49
00:03:13,565 --> 00:03:18,490
against 24.4 per cent
for the Liberal Socialist

50
00:03:18,570 --> 00:03:23,575
candidate and 14.5 per
cent for an independent.

51
00:03:23,575 --> 00:03:28,441
The PAP won 43 of the 51 seats
in the Legislative Assembly

52
00:03:28,521 --> 00:03:33,125
and formed Singapore's first
fully elected government.

53
00:03:33,125 --> 00:03:37,272
In September 1963, days after
Singapore joined Malaysia,

54
00:03:37,352 --> 00:03:41,047
Yaacob moved to the Southern
Islands constituency,

55
00:03:41,127 --> 00:03:44,142
which took in Blakang Mati (now Sentosa),

56
00:03:44,222 --> 00:03:48,525
Pulau Brani and the smaller
islands south of the harbour.

57
00:03:48,525 --> 00:03:52,822
The seat had been held since
1959 by Ahmad Jabri bin

58
00:03:52,902 --> 00:03:57,700
Akib of UMNO, who stood again
for the Singapore Alliance.

59
00:03:57,700 --> 00:04:02,189
Yaacob won it with 2,764 votes,

60
00:04:02,269 --> 00:04:04,775
or 55.4 per cent.

61
00:04:04,775 --> 00:04:08,528
The islanders lived mostly
in kampongs of fishermen,

62
00:04:08,608 --> 00:04:11,925
boatmen and workers for
the British military.

63
00:04:11,925 --> 00:04:16,386
Blakang Mati alone had about
1,000 residents by 1959,

64
00:04:16,466 --> 00:04:19,643
in two villages on its northern shore,

65
00:04:19,723 --> 00:04:23,150
one mainly Malay and one mainly Hokkien.

66
00:04:23,150 --> 00:04:27,363
Until 1959 they drew their
water from wells and springs or

67
00:04:27,443 --> 00:04:31,507
bought it from military
reservoirs, and in that year the

68
00:04:31,587 --> 00:04:36,250
British army laid a pipeline to
the island from Keppel Harbour.

69
00:04:36,250 --> 00:04:38,840
As their assemblyman, Yaacob worked to

70
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:41,800
improve living conditions on the islands.

71
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:46,299
By 1963 there were three
primary schools on Blakang

72
00:04:46,379 --> 00:04:50,250
Mati and Pulau Brani,
with about 750 pupils,

73
00:04:50,330 --> 00:04:54,550
as well as Islamic classes
for Muslim children.

74
00:04:54,550 --> 00:05:00,301
In 1964 the government built the
Blakang Mati Integrated Primary School,

75
00:05:00,381 --> 00:05:03,864
which brought the Malay,
English and Chinese

76
00:05:03,944 --> 00:05:08,237
streams together under one
roof at a cost of $175,000,

77
00:05:08,317 --> 00:05:13,500
and in July Yaacob officially
opened a new school on the island.

78
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:17,378
That year he also opened
a mosque on Pulau Sebarok

79
00:05:17,458 --> 00:05:21,575
for about 40 Muslim workers
of the oil company Esso.

80
00:05:21,575 --> 00:05:24,825
The island communities did not last.

81
00:05:24,825 --> 00:05:28,926
In the early 1970s Blakang
Mati was redeveloped as the

82
00:05:29,006 --> 00:05:33,184
leisure island of Sentosa,
and its villagers were moved

83
00:05:33,264 --> 00:05:37,600
to the mainland, many of them
to flats in Telok Blangah.

84
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:41,046
Some of the integrated
school's staff and pupils

85
00:05:41,126 --> 00:05:43,983
went to Telok Blangah Integrated School,

86
00:05:44,063 --> 00:05:48,250
and the old school site became
Sentosa's Maritime Museum.

87
00:05:48,250 --> 00:05:51,818
Other island villages were
cleared over the following

88
00:05:51,898 --> 00:05:55,535
decades for industry and,
in the case of Pulau Seking,

89
00:05:55,615 --> 00:05:58,575
the last of them, for
the Semakau landfill.

90
00:05:58,575 --> 00:06:02,725
Its settlement was demolished in 1993.

91
00:06:02,725 --> 00:06:07,800
Yaacob's most important years in
politics came during the merger.

92
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:12,485
In 1990 he was awarded the
Distinguished Service Order,

93
00:06:12,565 --> 00:06:18,809
after his death, for his vital role
in Singapore's political development,

94
00:06:18,889 --> 00:06:21,575
particularly from 1961 to 1965.

95
00:06:21,575 --> 00:06:25,168
These were years in which
Malay leaders in the PAP

96
00:06:25,248 --> 00:06:28,700
were often branded traitors
by their opponents.

97
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:32,878
On the 16th of July 1964
the Straits Times reported

98
00:06:32,958 --> 00:06:37,300
his appeal to Singapore's
Malays to "have patience".

99
00:06:37,300 --> 00:06:40,334
Five days later, on the 21st of July,

100
00:06:40,414 --> 00:06:43,275
race riots broke out in Singapore.

101
00:06:43,275 --> 00:06:48,525
Singapore left Malaysia
on the 9th of August 1965.

102
00:06:48,525 --> 00:06:52,135
Yaacob was deeply disappointed,
and he later summed up

103
00:06:52,215 --> 00:06:56,782
the moment with a Malay saying:
"The rice has turned into porridge",

104
00:06:56,862 --> 00:06:59,800
meaning that what is
done cannot be undone.

105
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:04,337
On the 15th of December 1965
he told the new Parliament

106
00:07:04,417 --> 00:07:08,950
that Singapore's Malays would
not migrate to Malaysia.

107
00:07:08,950 --> 00:07:12,500
"We Malays have never migrated," he said.

108
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:15,288
At Lee Kuan Yew's request the speech

109
00:07:15,368 --> 00:07:18,475
was broadcast on the radio three times.

110
00:07:18,475 --> 00:07:23,084
He served as Parliamentary Secretary
and then Minister of State in

111
00:07:23,164 --> 00:07:27,925
the Prime Minister's Office, and
later as Senior Minister of State.

112
00:07:27,925 --> 00:07:34,328
In 1968 he became the first MP for
the new Kampong Ubi constituency,

113
00:07:34,408 --> 00:07:37,650
winning 81.9 per cent of the vote.

114
00:07:37,650 --> 00:07:41,815
He held the seat in
1972, with 56.9 per cent

115
00:07:41,895 --> 00:07:46,831
against candidates from the
Singapore Malay National

116
00:07:46,911 --> 00:07:51,654
Organisation (PKMS) and
the United National Front,

117
00:07:51,734 --> 00:07:56,075
and in 1976, with 68.5
per cent against PKMS.

118
00:07:56,075 --> 00:08:00,450
Yaacob was a strong advocate
for the Malay community.

119
00:08:00,450 --> 00:08:03,796
He argued for recognition
of the special position of

120
00:08:03,876 --> 00:08:07,420
the Malays and for the
promotion of the Malay language,

121
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:11,241
held that Singapore's first
head of state should be Malay,

122
00:08:11,321 --> 00:08:14,550
and lobbied for Malay-medium
secondary education.

123
00:08:14,550 --> 00:08:18,812
He said that he helped the Malays
because they were the community

124
00:08:18,892 --> 00:08:23,288
"least advanced in Singapore", not
simply because they were Malays,

125
00:08:23,368 --> 00:08:26,775
and he remained a firm
supporter of multiracialism.

126
00:08:26,775 --> 00:08:29,944
He backed Sang Nila
Utama Secondary School,

127
00:08:30,024 --> 00:08:33,650
Singapore's first
Malay-medium secondary school.

128
00:08:33,650 --> 00:08:38,140
It was opened on Upper
Aljunied Road on the 14th

129
00:08:38,220 --> 00:08:42,520
of October 1961 by the
Minister for Education,

130
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:47,075
Yong Nyuk Lin, with 477
pupils and 18 teachers.

131
00:08:47,075 --> 00:08:52,108
Malay-medium pre-university
classes followed in 1965,

132
00:08:52,188 --> 00:08:56,625
and English-stream classes
were added in 1968.

133
00:08:56,625 --> 00:09:01,400
Enrolment fell as English became
the main language of schooling.

134
00:09:01,400 --> 00:09:06,094
The pre-university classes
ended in 1979, when the Ministry

135
00:09:06,174 --> 00:09:10,221
of Education phased out
pre-university centres that

136
00:09:10,301 --> 00:09:14,833
did not teach in English, only
two Malay-stream secondary

137
00:09:14,913 --> 00:09:19,525
classes were left by 1984,
and the school closed in 1988.

138
00:09:19,525 --> 00:09:24,126
Its buildings were demolished
for the Bidadari housing estate,

139
00:09:24,206 --> 00:09:27,070
and in 2019 a road there was named Sang

140
00:09:27,150 --> 00:09:30,850
Nila Utama Road at the
Malay community's request.

141
00:09:30,850 --> 00:09:35,244
Yaacob served as Singapore's
first ambassador to the

142
00:09:35,324 --> 00:09:39,890
Philippines from 1969 to
1971, and President Ferdinand

143
00:09:39,970 --> 00:09:44,100
Marcos decorated him with
the Order of Sikatuna.

144
00:09:44,100 --> 00:09:49,331
He was High Commissioner
to India from 1977 to 1984,

145
00:09:49,411 --> 00:09:53,700
and in 1984 he became ambassador to Egypt.

146
00:09:53,700 --> 00:09:57,445
In Cairo he drew attention
to the living conditions

147
00:09:57,525 --> 00:10:01,045
of Singapore students at
Al-Azhar University and

148
00:10:01,125 --> 00:10:04,950
started a fund-raising
drive for a hostel for them.

149
00:10:04,950 --> 00:10:09,775
He resigned in April 1986
because of poor health.

150
00:10:09,775 --> 00:10:12,525
He had a long link with Egypt.

151
00:10:12,525 --> 00:10:16,615
In 1962 President Gamal
Abdel Nasser awarded

152
00:10:16,695 --> 00:10:20,675
him the Order of the United Arab Republic.

153
00:10:20,675 --> 00:10:24,294
In 1981 the National
University of Singapore

154
00:10:24,374 --> 00:10:27,909
gave him an honorary
Doctor of Laws for his

155
00:10:27,989 --> 00:10:32,025
contribution to a harmonious
multiracial nation.

156
00:10:32,025 --> 00:10:35,895
After he left Parliament,
Yaacob kept speaking at public

157
00:10:35,975 --> 00:10:39,925
forums and writing letters
to Malay-language newspapers.

158
00:10:39,925 --> 00:10:42,807
He kept good relations
with the government,

159
00:10:42,887 --> 00:10:46,388
but he believed that citizens
had the right to offer

160
00:10:46,468 --> 00:10:50,050
constructive criticism of
its policies without fear.

161
00:10:50,050 --> 00:10:52,601
"To compromise is not to give

162
00:10:52,681 --> 00:10:55,675
in," he said in a 1986 interview.

163
00:10:55,675 --> 00:10:59,900
He also served as deputy president
of the Asian Sepak Takraw

164
00:10:59,980 --> 00:11:05,280
Federation and president of the Singapore
Amateur Sepak Takraw Association,

165
00:11:05,360 --> 00:11:08,652
was involved in the
Singapore Silat Federation,

166
00:11:08,732 --> 00:11:12,813
and helped to set up the Lembaga
Biasiswa Kenangan Maulud,

167
00:11:12,893 --> 00:11:16,050
a scholarship fund for
needy Malay students.

168
00:11:16,050 --> 00:11:21,525
In August 1989 he suffered a
heart attack in Kuala Lumpur.

169
00:11:21,525 --> 00:11:25,257
He died of leukaemia at
the Singapore General

170
00:11:25,338 --> 00:11:29,155
Hospital on the 11th of
October 1989, aged 64,

171
00:11:29,235 --> 00:11:33,725
and was buried in the Muslim
cemetery at Jalan Bahar.

172
00:11:33,725 --> 00:11:39,125
He left his wife, Hasmah binti
Awang, three sons and five daughters.

173
00:11:39,125 --> 00:11:43,967
A biography in Malay by Sulaiman
Jeem and Abdul Ghani Hamid

174
00:11:44,047 --> 00:11:48,556
followed in 1990, and in 1992
a scholarship and bursary

175
00:11:48,636 --> 00:11:53,475
in his name were set up for
needy students in Kampong Ubi.

176
00:11:53,475 --> 00:11:57,313
Why it matters today: Yaacob
Mohamed spent his political

177
00:11:57,393 --> 00:12:01,022
life arguing that standing
up for the Malay community

178
00:12:01,102 --> 00:12:04,950
and building a multiracial
Singapore could go together.

179
00:12:04,950 --> 00:12:09,026
His move from UMNO in 1958
helped the PAP win Malay

180
00:12:09,107 --> 00:12:12,939
support at a decisive
time, and he spoke for the

181
00:12:13,019 --> 00:12:17,175
Malays in Parliament through
merger and separation.

182
00:12:17,175 --> 00:12:21,291
The Malay-medium school he
championed and the island villages

183
00:12:21,371 --> 00:12:25,487
he represented have both
disappeared, which may be one reason

184
00:12:25,567 --> 00:12:29,625
his name is less familiar than
those of his contemporaries.
